  Actually, the problem is more like:  "Here's my 'shopping
list' of strange things I'll need for various arcane rites."

  Most of Nethack's problems/situations are best solved by
knowing them and their solutions perfectly before you start,
and preparing long in advance.  

  Some problems/goals and their solution requirements interact
with others.  

  Gaining stats and experience levels are like that.  Higher Con 
and Wis will give you bigger Hp/Pw gains when you gain an 
experience level.  

  Higher Cha gives you a better chance of gaining an experience
level when interacting with foocubi.

  And you can keep benefitting from more "experience" even if
your XP doesn't increase.

  I still find this whole interdependent process interesting
even while totally spoiled about it.  Which is good because
almost the only way of doing it at all efficiently requires 
being totally spoiled.



  I think the focus of Nethack should be more on exploring and
learning than grinding determination get the perfect AK to Ascend
or nothing.

  Spoilers are called that for a reason, they spoil a big part
of what the game could be.


  Imagine a group of friends that all played Nethack but didn't
have (or maybe didn't want) access to the internet, spoilers,
rgrn, etc.  Sharing specific information about what items can
do might be more fun than actually playing the game.  (Watch
the #nethack irc channel to get an idea of what I'm talking 
about.)

  They'd start making lists of important things/effects, like 
instadeaths.  Explore mode and stethoscopes could be used to 
assess weapons damage/armor effectiveness.  Effects of armor on 
spellcasting would get noticed.

  They might never learn about buying divine protection.
But if they did, wow!  But once again, the most likely way
they'd learn about it would be by going into wizard/explore mode
and exhaustively trying different donation levels.  That's
a research project, not a game.

  Someone would start source-diving, and eventually they'd have
what we have now with rgrn/spoilers.  Except they'd have had
the fun of getting there.  


  (I imagine this is kind of like what actually happened.  I like
the scenario even if it didn't happen that way.  ;-)


  Another part of the problem is that once you know about
Ascending in Netahck, *that's what you want to do*.  Exploring
the neat stuff all through the game is brutally shunted
aside in favor of strip-mining every last spoiler to make 
ascension easier.  (Like I did to maximize gains from experience.
;^)


  The many conduct ascensions, as well as Marvin's track record,
show that careful play is the key to Ascending.  One husband
and wife team already posted that they play Nethack better 
together than they do apart.  

  I can imagine a group of AD&Ders getting together to play
Nethack when they can no longer find someone with time to DM.  
Looking over the shoulder of the typist also means they're
watching his/her back.  (In more ways than one. ;^)

  Hopefully this would slow down play enough to be effective.
Especially with people grabbing the typist's wrists shouting
"Stop fighting the minotaur, we're almost dead!"

  (Also it might keep the typist's fingers from killing the 
character.  Most of my deaths occur when my brain's not really
paying attention and my fingers are living their own life,
wild and free.  Anyone want to check out the left/right brain
implications of that?)

  With no spoilers there'd be less obsession with the absolute
best artifact weapon, armor, tools, damage, etc.  (Speed 
ascensions have shown 

  Getting Grayswandir might be a once in a lifetime achievement.
There's no problem with that.  Played unspoiled, Nethack could
easily be enjoyed for a lifetime without learning all its 
secrets.

  Ascension, if and when it happened, would be a tremendous
surprise (also a bit of a letdown.  As someone posted, "Is that
all there is?  When I saw the 'DYWYPI?' prompt I thought my
character had died.")  Nethack could easily record a separate 
file of screen shots of each level, what was achieved/acquired 
when, when the first of each monster class was killed, major 
monster kills, etc. that would be great for remembering great 
games.  


  So a big part of the problem is the "Ascension or Nothing"
attitude.  Part of the reason for that are other, lesser goals 
in the game, other than staying alive.


  Another problem is that, perhaps as a result of the public
player focus on Ascension and score, the difficulty level of 
the game seems adjusted for the Marvins of the world.  Which 
leads to more demand for spoiling the game.


  Don't get me wrong.  I like Nethack the way it is.  (I play
it enough. ;^)  But I'd like to find a way to make its rich
environment more accessible and satisfying, and less frustrating,
to people who *aren't* top players.  

  Some kind of limited goals that permit starting and finishing 
an hour, a few hours, and a day of play.  There's definitely 
enough substance in Nethack to make that possible.


